<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

`self_partner` is a course-local question type developed for MIT 6.390,
Introduction to Machine Learning.  It lets students form their own groups:
one student creates a named group, and the other students join it.

This is a useful case study because it looks like a question and uses
CAT-SOOP's submission controls, but its real result is a change to shared group
state rather than a graded answer.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Source Layout and Responsibilities</catsoop-section>

The qtype is installed in the course as:

```
__QTYPES__/
└── self_partner/
    ├── self_partner.py
    └── __STATIC__/
        └── self_partner.js
```

The Python file:

* reads the groups for the current page;
* validates create, join, and leave requests;
* uses a local lock around the final group-log overwrite;
* renders the current group and the available actions; and
* reports a message after each action.

The JavaScript file improves that server-rendered interface.  It exposes
Create, Join, and Leave controls, then turns those actions into clicks on
CAT-SOOP's normal Submit button.  The browser code does not decide whether a
group change is allowed; the Python code validates the operation again.

The asset is requested through `extra_headers`:

<pre><code class="language-python">def extra_headers(info):
    return (
        '&lt;script type="text/javascript" '
        'src="COURSE/__QTYPES__/self_partner/self_partner.js"&gt;'
        '&lt;/script&gt;'
    )</code></pre>

CAT-SOOP rewrites `COURSE` to the URL of the current course.

<catsoop-section>How the Question Is Used</catsoop-section>

The first lab places the qtype after instructions asking students to form
groups of two or three:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question self_partner&gt;
csq_display_name = "Select your partner"
csq_npoints = 0
csq_description = "Select your partner"
csq_name = "Lab_self_partner"
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

Each setting has a specific role:

* `self_partner` selects
  `__QTYPES__/self_partner/self_partner.py`.
* `csq_name` supplies the stable question, form, and log identifier.  This
  qtype's generated control names are derived from `Lab_self_partner`.
* `csq_display_name` labels the interaction in CAT-SOOP's surrounding question
  interface.
* `csq_npoints = 0` makes clear to course authors that choosing partners is not
  graded.  The qtype's own `total_points` function also returns zero.
* `csq_description` records a useful description, although this particular
  renderer does not put it directly into its custom HTML.

The page tells one member to create the group and send its full generated name
to the other members.  The server appends four random digits to the requested
base name.  The remaining members join by entering that exact full name.  The
qtype limits a group to three members.

Because the qtype passes `cs_path_info` to the group API, these groups belong
to the page on which the question appears.  Downstream course code retrieves a
student's group using the same path and the `"default"` section:

```python
context = csm_loader.generate_context(lab_path)
section, group, members = csm_groups.get_group(
    context,
    lab_path,
    username,
    secnum="default",
)
```

Using a different page path would address a different collection of groups.

<catsoop-section>The Submitted Actions</catsoop-section>

The qtype renders three raw form fields derived from `csq_name`:

```
__Lab_self_partner_0000    requested base name for a new group
__Lab_self_partner_0001    full name of a group to join
__Lab_self_partner_0002    the leave-group command
```

CAT-SOOP associates these prefixed fields with the parent question.  The
submission handler checks which field contains an action:

```python
name = info["csq_name"]
create = submissions.get("__%s_0000" % name, {}).get("data", "")
join = submissions.get("__%s_0001" % name, {}).get("data", "")
leave = submissions.get("__%s_0002" % name, {}).get("data", "")

if join:
    # Validate the group, its size, and current membership; then join it.
    ...
elif leave == "remove":
    # Remove cs_username from the current group.
    ...
else:
    # Validate create, generate a unique suffix, and create the group.
    ...
```

For creation, the implementation requires a base name of at least four
characters containing only letters, numbers, and underscores.  It truncates
the base to 40 characters and adds an underscore plus four random digits.  For
joining, it checks that the named group exists and has fewer than three
members.  Joining another group first removes the student from any previous
group.

An action response has the normal qtype shape:

```python
return {
    "score": 1,
    "msg": "You have joined the group.",
}
```

The success score lets the submission complete normally, but it contributes no
points because `total_points` returns zero.

<catsoop-section>Group Storage and Concurrency</catsoop-section>

The implementation reads group data with:

```python
groups = csm_groups.list_groups(globals(), cs_path_info)
```

The resulting mapping is organized by section, group name, and members:

```python
{
    "default": {
        "linear_models_4821": ["alice", "bob"],
    },
}
```

Logically, the data live in CAT-SOOP's `_groups` log database at the current
page path, in a log named `groups`.  With filesystem logging, that corresponds
to a location of this form:

```
_logs/_courses/COURSE/_groups/PAGE/PATH/groups.log
```

Group creation and joining are read-modify-write operations, and two students
can submit at nearly the same time.  The original course code contains a local
`my_overwrite_groups` workaround around an older logging issue.  It constructs
a replacement section, locks a file in `/tmp`, re-reads the top-level group
mapping, and writes that replacement section.

That lock protects the final overwrite, but the replacement section was
computed before the lock was acquired.  It therefore does not make the whole
membership check and update atomic.  New qtypes should prefer the current
lock-aware `csm_groups` or `csm_cslog` operations rather than copying the
workaround.  A hard-coded lock in `/tmp` also coordinates only processes that
share the same filesystem, can couple otherwise unrelated courses, and does
not cover a multi-server deployment.  Shared deployments need locking supplied
by the shared log backend.

For more about the general log layout, see
[Reading CAT-SOOP Logs](CURRENT/../../authoring/logs) and
[Problem Logs and Question Metadata](CURRENT/../../authoring/problem_logs).

<catsoop-section>Rendering and Browser Behavior</catsoop-section>

On each full page render, the qtype reads the current group state.  A student
who is not in a group sees controls to create a group or join an existing one.
A student who is already in a group sees the group name, its members, and a
Leave button.  The submission response contains a freshly constructed status
message; a later page reload reconstructs the full interface from the group
log.

The JavaScript:

* hides generic question UI that is irrelevant to an ungraded group chooser,
  including the score, solution, attempts display, and standard button row;
* shows qtype-specific buttons;
* submits the real CAT-SOOP form when a student presses Enter or activates one
  of those buttons; and
* writes `"remove"` into the hidden leave field before submitting a leave
  request.

This is progressive enhancement of CAT-SOOP's submission mechanism, not an
authorization boundary.  Membership checks and limits remain in Python.

<catsoop-section>Lessons for New Stateful Question Types</catsoop-section>

`self_partner` demonstrates several useful techniques:

* a qtype can represent an action or workflow even when it is worth no points;
* secondary controls can use names derived from one stable `csq_name`;
* `extra_headers` can load course-local JavaScript;
* course modules such as `csm_groups` and `csm_cslog` are available to the
  qtype.

It also highlights improvements to make when generalizing a one-course qtype:

* escape every group name and username before inserting it into HTML;
* use a cryptographically suitable or collision-resistant identifier if a
  group name is security-sensitive;
* scope locks correctly and use a backend that works across all application
  servers;
* remove empty groups consistently;
* include `csq_name` in every DOM ID and namespace the JavaScript instead of
  assuming only one instance appears on a page; and
* make the section name and maximum group size settings such as
  `csq_section` and `csq_max_group_size`, rather than hard-coded constants.

Those changes preserve the original interaction while making it safer to
reuse on several pages, in several courses, or more than once on one page.

For the basic qtype interface behind this example, see
[Designing Custom Question Types](CURRENT/../qtypes).
